    {
      "title": "Police, protesters clash in Paris as 410,000 demonstrate across France",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/police-protesters-clash-in-paris-as-410-000-demonstrate-across-france-126102401095_1.html",
      "description": "Protesters and police are clashing in Paris, with tear gas fired and projectiles thrown, at the tail end of demonstrations in the capital and elsewhere that the French government says have drawn 410,000 demonstrators nationwide.\nParis saw the largest protest on Saturday, with the interior ministry estimating that 110,000 took part in a march on the city's southeastern edge that started peacefully but is ending with violence.\nPolice have fired volleys of tear gas and are also using water cannons to try to disperse people, including some who have thrown projectiles.",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-24T16:44:52"
    },
    {
      "title": "Mass protests challenge Macron as French police fire tear gas, use cannons",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/mass-protests-challenge-macron-as-french-police-fire-tear-gas-use-cannons-126102401075_1.html",
      "description": "More than 100,000 people marched in Paris on Saturday over school funding and soaring living costs, challenging President Emmanuel Macron's government as police fired tear gas and water cannons near the end of the protest.\nParis police estimated turnout at 110,000, with demonstrators mobilizing despite restrictions that confined the march to the city's southeastern edge, away from the center.\nPolice used tear gas in several cities, including Lyon, Clermont-Ferrand, and Strasbourg, and water cannons in Nice and Nantes, French media reported. In Marseille, officers fired tear gas during clashes with several dozen people near the march's endpoint.\nThe Paris prosecutor's office said 64 people had been arrested in connection with Saturday's demonstration and 40 placed in police custody. Those arrested included six minors. Most arrests concerned suspected weapons offenses or participation in a group preparing violence or property damage, the office said.\nThe unrest is testing Macron's ..",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-24T16:30:44"
    }
  ]
}